Subject: [PATCH] config: provide non-empty kernel fallback for non-Linux uname

When platform.uname() returns a release string that does not start with
[0-9.]+ (e.g. OHOS reports 'HongMeng Kernel 1.12.0', OpenBSD reports
'13.0', WSL reports '4.4.0-19041-Microsoft'), parse_kernel_version()
returns [] as documented in tests/test_unit/test_config.py. Several
consumers assume a non-empty list and index config.kernel[0] directly:
  - pyroute2/netlink/nlsocket.py:843 (NetlinkSocket.capabilities)
  - pyroute2/ipdb/interfaces.py:289 (legacy kernel-3 branch)

Off-platform unit tests that do not actually drive netlink I/O
(e.g. test_unit/test_entry_points/test_basic.py::test_exceptions,
test_unit/test_iproute_match/test_match.py::test_get_leaf[...]) raise
IndexError at fixture setup time when this happens.

Fall back to a high default ([99, 0, 0]) so the IndexError is avoided
without changing the semantics of parse_kernel_version() — the unit
test_config.py::test_kernel_version still expects [] for 'test.10.12'.
[99, 0, 0] is interpreted as "very new" by all current consumers.

--- a/pyroute2/config/__init__.py
+++ b/pyroute2/config/__init__.py
@@ -42,6 +42,14 @@
 machine = platform.machine()
 arch = platform.architecture()[0]
 kernel = parse_kernel_version(uname[2])
+# Portability: parse_kernel_version() returns [] for platform.uname()
+# releases that don't start with [0-9.]+ (e.g. OHOS reports
+# 'HongMeng Kernel 1.12.0', OpenBSD reports '13.0', etc.). Several
+# consumers assume a non-empty list and index config.kernel[0]
+# directly (see nlsocket.NetlinkSocket.capabilities, ipdb.interfaces).
+# Fall back to a high default so off-platform unit tests that do not
+# actually drive netlink I/O do not IndexError.
+kernel = kernel or [99, 0, 0]

 AF_BRIDGE = getattr(socket, 'AF_BRIDGE', 7)
 AF_NETLINK = getattr(socket, 'AF_NETLINK', 16)
--
2.39.0
